WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.436
-Our first guest
is a four-time Oscar nominee

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:05.638
starring in the acclaimed
television series,

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:09.141
"The Good Lord Bird," which is
available now on Showtime.

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:11.678
Here is Ethan Hawke, everybody!

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.745
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:14.146
That's what I'm talking about.

00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:15.815
That's my man.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.283
[ Chuckling ] Yeah.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:18.885
That's what I'm
talking about, buddy.

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:20.586
It's good to see you.
and thank you so much

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:23.956
for coming on our show.
It means a lot to us.

00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:26.792
-Well, I wish I could be there
in New York in real life,

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:28.861
you know. I mean, we're through
this pandemic thing,

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:30.863
or it's at least
in the side-view mirror.

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:32.732
So I wish I could be there
and say hi to Tariq

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:33.866
and say hi to everybody else.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:35.568
-Yeah, exactly.
Well, you will be back.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.103
Next time,
we'll do it in person.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.538
We have to.
-Yes.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.173
-I'm just happy you're here,
'cause I want to talk to you.

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:43.843
Last time, actually, you were
nice enough to do our show

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.178
when we were doing it from home,

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:46.579
and we didn't really know
what we were doing.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.114
We were just doing
something just to --

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.415
Yeah, everyone just --
We just figured

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:50.649
we'd just put
something out there

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.252
and see if anyone needs
something to look at,

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:53.585
so thank you for doing that.

00:00:53.585 --> 00:00:57.190
And my girls jumped in
and just crashed the interview

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.925
and thought you were so funny

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.960
and just wanted to just,
like, interview --

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.495 line:0%
like, interrupt the interview.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:05.631 line:0%
They just jumped in, and
you were like, "Oh, my gosh."

00:01:05.631 --> 00:01:08.134
But thank you. You obviously
know how to deal with kids.

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.802
You were cool with them.
You were making them laugh.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:12.505
-Well, they were great,
and those were dark days.

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:15.275
I mean, I was thinking back
about that interview

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:17.309
and what a long,
strange year it's been.

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:18.945
-Yeah.
-We were just, kind of,

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.847
you know, we were juggling
in thin air there,

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:22.615
trying to create
some kind of entertainment

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:23.650
when there was none.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.551
-Yeah, exactly. Right, yeah.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:26.852
Right now, are you in --

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:29.088
Where are you?
You're in Budapest?

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:32.492
-Okay, well, this amazing
background I have,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.393
the thing about
the life of an actor

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.896
is you spend most of your life
in beige hotel rooms

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:39.398
and beige apartments.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:40.866
-That's interesting.

00:01:40.866 --> 00:01:45.671
-It is very close to midnight
here in Budapest, Hungary.

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:49.108
And I'm here.
I've signed about 10,000 NDAs.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:51.377
I'm not allowed to say
what I'm here doing.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.679
I will say that
it's not for the government.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:55.048
I'm not doing anything illicit.

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:57.016
I'm making quality entertainment

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.151
for a studio known as Marvel.

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:01.554
But they're very serious --
-That is what I'm talking about.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:05.691
That's what I'm
talking about, buddy.

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.827
Don't say anything. I don't want
to get you in any trouble.

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.229
But I appreciate you
even just staying up late

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.731
and doing the show for us.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.766
And I know you want to give a
shout-out to Tariq,

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:17.303
because you guys
worked together.

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.739
I got to say,
it was "Waiting for Godot,"

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.740
and Tariq told me about this.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.343
I couldn't believe it.
Thank you for doing it.

00:02:24.343 --> 00:02:28.548
It's available still online
if you go thenewgroup.org.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:31.016
How did you come up with it?

00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:32.218
And what was the purpose?

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.686
-What was the idea?
-Yeah.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.087
-Well, the thought was this.

00:02:35.087 --> 00:02:37.957
Right around the time that
your kids ruined my interview

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.326
and humiliated me in public
in front of the nation...

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.061
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:45.798
...none of us had anything
to do, and so we didn't know.

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.099
You know, I have four kids,

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.868
and so I thought one night,
I know what we'll do.

00:02:49.868 --> 00:02:51.637
We'll read "Waiting for Godot."

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:54.139
Now, the kids were not
as into this as I was,

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:55.508
but it was incredibly fun.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:59.078
We lit a fire,
and we read this play,

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:01.180
and I was kind of knocked out.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.582
You know, there is something
about this experience.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:04.617
Even right now, you know,
here I am on Zoom still,

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.320
and there is something
so alienating about it

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.155
that the play seemed alive.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.623
All my life, it seemed like

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:13.058
some existential,
intellectual exercise.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:15.695
And all of a sudden,
I thought it was so funny.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:17.629
And we were all --

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:19.365
You know, I mean you've
heard a lot said about this.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:22.601
But musicians and actors,
the theater,

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.337
have taken a real
kick in the teeth

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.372
like a lot of people have
in the last year.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:30.009
And so this is Tariq, Wally
Shawn, John Leguizamo and I

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:32.311
decided to go to war
for the theater.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:35.515
And we did this production of
"Waiting for Godot" over Zoom.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.016
And you can download it,
watch it,

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.152
and at least it's not going to
be the same boring production

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:40.353
that you saw in high school.

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:41.921
It will be a little bit better.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.990
-Or maybe it won't,
but at least we tried.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.391
-No, it's unbelievable.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.527
I don't even know
how you pulled it off.

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:50.329
Because, again,
we were all in lockdown.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:52.431
And it was like, "Okay,
I don't know what --"

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.401
I thought Tariq was saying that
he was gonna go rehearse this.

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.703
And he was rehearsing,
but it was all for online.

00:03:57.703 --> 00:03:59.872
I was like, "How are you
pulling this off?"

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:03.609
Like, you had to light it
yourself and kind of --

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.044
It was amazing.
-Well, yeah.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:09.282
Listen, I mean, I had to build
the set in my son's bedroom

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:12.551
with my wife while we had COVID.
Alright?

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:15.621
-Holy moly.
-So it was a dark day.

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:18.090
-Wow!
-But we did it.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.992
Tariq was there
every step of the way.

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.828
This guy was money every day.

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:23.363
-Nah, listen -- Listen, man,

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.931
like, the fact that you guys
pushed through

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:27.733
and, you know,
really came through

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:31.004
and stuck with the project.
Even, like, with COVID

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:33.039
and having to, you know,
like, multitask

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:34.540
in the way that you did,
it was --

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.076
It really set the precedent
for me for professionalism.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:38.377
And now I look at The Roots.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:39.912
I'm like, "[Scoffs]
These guys --"

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.314
No, I'm just joking.

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.348
No. No.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:43.916
-He said the same thing about
you.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.985
Class act, dude. You know
how to -- That's a pro.

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:47.720
-Right.
-I want to talk about --

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.123
Speaking of being a pro,
"The Good Lord Bird,"

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:55.394
man, you were, again, Ethan,
I love you but unrecognizable.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:57.697
And I go how did --
It's based on the book.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:00.533
How did you hear about the book?

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:03.202
-Well, I was doing a scene
with Denzel Washington

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:07.807
in "Magnificent Seven" and
the camera operator said to me,

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.975
"You know, you should
play John Brown."

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:12.411
And I remember thinking,
"What makes you say that?"

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:13.546
And he said,
"I'm reading this book

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.581
called 'The Good Lord Bird.'"
and my --

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.350
You know, your brain
gets obsessed with something.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.452
I couldn't figure out
what that title was.

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.921
And he said, "No,
it's when you see a bird,

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:25.024
and it's so beautiful
that you just say, 'Good Lord!'"

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:26.658
And so then the title
stuck in my head,

00:05:26.658 --> 00:05:28.961
and I was in an airport,
and I picked it up,

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:31.030
and I just laughed my ass off.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.999
And my wife said to me,

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:34.233
you know, "What are you
laughing about?"

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:35.401
And I said, "This book."

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:37.303
And she said, "Isn't it
about John Brown?"

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:38.837
And I said, "Yeah."

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:41.440
And that's kind of the miracle
that McBride pulled off.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.478
He manages to talk about
some of the sins of this nation

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:47.079
and the foundation
of this nation

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:50.049
and Harriet Tubman and Frederick
Douglass and John Brown

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:53.385
in a way
that knocks you sideways.

00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:56.822
It's -- The story's told with so
much love and so much humor

00:05:56.822 --> 00:06:00.893
and so much silliness
that the --

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:03.362
In the novel, anyway,
it really opens your heart.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:06.765
And so our goal was to do
the same thing with movies.

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:08.701
-You change your look.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.202
You changed your voice.

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.772
Pretty un-- I want to show
a picture of you here.

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:13.839
This is what
you look like there.

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:15.707
And look at this.

00:06:15.707 --> 00:06:17.476
You knocked it out of the park
yet again, bud.

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:18.810
Congrats on
all the great reviews.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.379
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:22.581
Here's Ethan Hawke
in "The Good Lord Bird."

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:24.082
Take a look at this.

00:06:24.082 --> 00:06:28.621
-My name is Captain John Brown!

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:33.992
And I am here in the name
of the Great Redeemer!

00:06:33.992 --> 00:06:38.264
The King of Kings!
The man of the holy trinity!

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:44.336
And I hereby order you to git!

00:06:44.336 --> 00:06:48.006
Git in his holy name! Git!

00:06:48.006 --> 00:06:50.609
For he is on the side
of justice,

00:06:50.609 --> 00:06:53.779
and you are
on the side of change!

00:06:53.779 --> 00:06:55.414
-Brown!

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:56.448
[ Guns cock ]

00:06:56.448 --> 00:07:01.286
Death is comin' for ya today!

00:07:01.286 --> 00:07:03.189
[ Grunts ]

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:05.191
[ Yells ]

00:07:08.027 --> 00:07:11.264
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:13.432
-Wow!

00:07:13.432 --> 00:07:14.533
Wow!

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:16.769
Imagine having to do
two takes of that!

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:17.803
I mean, come on.

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:19.471
Unbelievable. You are the man.

00:07:19.471 --> 00:07:20.773
Ethan Hawke
right there everybody.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:23.709
"The Good Lord Bird"
is available now on Showtime.

